Hi Chaoyi,

You are right, and it is wider than this patch. Nothing consumes npu-supply.
There is no regulator call in drivers/accel/rocket, and grepping the tree for
npu-supply and sram-supply finds no reader at all, so the RK3588 boards that
set them are in the same position. The property is required by
rockchip,rk3588-rknn-core.yaml, so a dts cannot simply drop it either.

Your suspicion is what the always-on line in this patch is for. Without it the
regulator core turns an unclaimed rail off when it disables unused regulators,
so that line is the only thing holding vdd_npu_s0 up here.

I went to move the rail onto the power domain instead, since domain-supply is
the one mechanism that has a consumer, and rk3588-rock-5b-5bp-5t.dtsi already
wires the same rail that way. On the board that turned out not to be enough,
which I would rather report than repeat as a plan.

  vdd_npu_s0  state=enabled  num_users=0     at boot
  vdd_npu_s0  state=enabled  num_users=0     after an inference

With always-on dropped and domain-supply on the NPU domain, nothing takes the
rail. rockchip_pd_regulator_enable returns before it looks at the property
unless the domain has need_regulator, that flag is only set by DOMAIN_M_R and
DOMAIN_RK3399, and RK3576 domains are built by a macro that does not carry the
field, so it is zero for all of them. The rail stays up because boot-on left it
up and nothing claimed it, not because anything is managing it.

So this needs the domain flag as well as the wiring, and that is a change to a
shared table rather than to a board file. I am testing that combination before
I put it in a patch.

One more thing to withdraw while I am here. This commit message says that
dropping the rail under the domains takes an async SError on the next power-on.
I went looking for the run that showed it and cannot find one. Every SError I
have recorded here is the power domain itself, which is what patches 6 and 7
address, and I think I attributed it to the rail from memory. That sentence is
out of v8 whatever the test says.

Thanks for looking at it, the question turned out to be worth more than the
answer I had ready.

